<Legal title="Privacy policy" description="How opendesk collects, uses, shares, keeps and deletes personal data, including data from Google and Microsoft accounts you connect, and the rights you have." updated="October 19, 2026" toc={toc} email={EMAIL}>
  <p>This policy explains what personal data opendesk collects, why, who we share it with, how long we keep it and how to have it deleted. We have tried to write it plainly. If anything is unclear, write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="who-we-are">Who we are</h2>
  <p>opendesk is operated by {COMPANY.legalName} ("opendesk", "we", "us"), a company registered in {COMPANY.registeredIn} with company number {COMPANY.number}. Our registered office is at {COMPANY.address}. We are registered with the Information Commissioner's Office as a data protection fee payer, registration reference {COMPANY.ico}.</p>
  <p>For the data described in this policy about our own customers and website visitors, we are the controller. For data our customers put into their workspaces, see the next section.</p>

  <h2 id="scope">Scope</h2>
  <p>This policy covers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>this website, opendesk.sh, and our documentation at docs.opendesk.sh;</li>
    <li>opendesk Cloud: the web app at app.opendesk.sh, the API at api.opendesk.sh, the realtime service, help centers and the chat widget hosted by us (together, "the Service").</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Customer data.</strong> Our customers (the organizations that create workspaces) use the Service to talk to their own customers. Tickets, messages, contacts, attachments and articles in a workspace are customer data. For customer data we act as a processor on behalf of the workspace owner, who is the controller. If you contacted a company that uses opendesk, that company decides how your data is used; please contact them first. We will help them answer you.</p>
  <p><strong>Self-hosted opendesk.</strong> opendesk is open-source software. If an organization runs it on its own servers, we do not receive, host or have access to any of that data. The organization running it is responsible for it, and this policy does not apply to it.</p>

  <h2 id="what-we-collect">What we collect</h2>
  <h3>Account data</h3>
  <p>When you sign up or are invited: your name, email address, password (stored only as an Argon2id hash), profile picture if you add one, two-factor settings, and the workspaces and roles you belong to. If you sign in with Google, Microsoft, GitHub or another identity provider, we receive your name, email address, profile picture and a stable account identifier from that provider.</p>
  <h3>Workspace content</h3>
  <p>What you and your team create or receive in the Service: tickets, email messages and their attachments, chat conversations, internal notes, contacts and companies, tags, saved replies, views, automations, SLA policies, knowledge base articles, webhooks and settings.</p>
  <h3>Connected mailboxes</h3>
  <p>When a workspace connects a mailbox, we receive the email the workspace forwards from that mailbox to an opendesk address, so it can be shown as tickets, and we send the replies agents write from the mailbox's address. Depending on how the mailbox is connected, replies are sent through the Gmail API or Microsoft Graph, or through the SMTP server the workspace chose: its own mail server or a sending service. A send-only Google or Microsoft connection lets us send email from that address and tells us the account's email address; it does not let us read the mailbox's contents. A workspace on the Collab plan can instead connect a support mailbox to read its inbox directly, so new email becomes tickets without forwarding; that connection reads the inbox's new email and can mark it read, label and archive it as the workspace's settings say. We store the credentials needed to keep the connection working (an SMTP or IMAP password, or OAuth access and refresh tokens), encrypted. The sections on <a href="#google">Google</a> and <a href="#microsoft">Microsoft</a> data say more.</p>
  <h3>Collab mail accounts</h3>
  <p>On the Collab plan, a member can connect their own Google, Microsoft or IMAP email account to read and handle it in opendesk like a mail app. We then copy that account's email, folders and labels into opendesk and keep them in sync both ways: reading, starring, labelling, moving, archiving, trashing and sending in opendesk is done at the provider too, and changes made elsewhere come back. We also read and, when the member changes them, update the account's signature, sender name, automatic replies and categories. A private account and its email are visible only to the member who connected it; workspace admins cannot see it. A shared account is visible only to the people and teams it is shared with. Collab mail never becomes tickets and is never sent to AI providers.</p>
  <h3>Chat widget visitors</h3>
  <p>When someone uses a workspace's chat widget, we process what they type and send, any name or email address they give, an identifier the website may pass to verify who they are, a visitor token stored in their browser so the conversation continues, and their browser's user agent.</p>
  <h3>Usage and device data</h3>
  <p>For security and to run the Service, we record IP addresses and user agents for sign-in sessions, and keep an audit log of security events such as sign-ins, failed sign-ins, password and two-factor changes, role changes, API key and webhook changes, and mailbox connections. Request logs record the route, status and timing of requests; they never include request bodies, query strings, passwords or tokens.</p>
  <h3>Website analytics</h3>
  <p>On this website we may use PostHog to count page views. It runs without cookies or other storage in your browser, does not create a profile of you, and does not record your session. In the app, we use PostHog to understand which features are used and to find errors. Analytics events never contain message content, secrets or credentials.</p>
  <h3>Billing</h3>
  <p>For paid plans we keep the plan, number of agents, billing contact and invoices. At checkout we collect your company's legal name, billing address and, where you give one, its VAT or other tax number, so they appear on your invoices. Payments are handled by Stripe, which collects and stores card details; we never see or store full card numbers.</p>
  <h3>Communications</h3>
  <p>If you email us, we keep the conversation so we can help you.</p>

  <h2 id="how-we-use">How we use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>To provide the Service: show tickets, receive forwarded email, deliver replies, run automations and SLAs, send notifications and keep everyone's screen up to date.</li>
    <li>To secure the Service: authenticate you, prevent abuse and fraud, rate limit, and investigate incidents.</li>
    <li>To send service email: verification, password resets, invitations, notifications you can control, and important notices about your account.</li>
    <li>To support you when you ask for help.</li>
    <li>To run the AI features you use, as described under <a href="#ai">AI features</a>.</li>
    <li>To improve the Service, using aggregated usage information.</li>
    <li>To bill paid plans and meet our legal obligations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We do not sell personal data. We do not use customer data or email content for advertising, and we do not use it to train generalized artificial intelligence or machine learning models.</p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI features</h2>
  <p>opendesk includes optional AI features: reply drafts, rewriting, summaries, a copilot that answers questions about a ticket, auto-triage, translation, an answer bot in the chat widget and semantic search. Workspace admins can turn them off for the whole workspace.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What is sent.</strong> When someone uses an AI feature, or an admin turns on an automatic one such as auto-triage or the answer bot, we send the AI provider only the content that feature needs, for example the messages in a ticket, relevant knowledge base articles and your workspace's tone settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Why.</strong> Customer content is sent to the AI provider only to perform the feature that was requested, and the result is returned to your workspace.</li>
    <li><strong>No training.</strong> Customer content and AI outputs are not used to train AI models, by us or by our AI providers.</li>
    <li><strong>No logging of content.</strong> We do not log prompt or completion text. We record only usage data, such as which feature ran, on which model, how many tokens it used and what it cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Providers.</strong> On opendesk Cloud, AI features run on AI providers we contract with, under terms that forbid training on your content and limit them to providing the feature.</li>
    <li><strong>The answer bot</strong> replies only from a workspace's published help center articles, is labelled as AI, and offers a way to reach a person.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Email in Collab mail accounts is never sent to AI providers. Email a support mailbox receives becomes tickets, which the AI features above handle like any other ticket.</p>
  <p>Self-hosted opendesk uses whichever AI provider its operator configures, or none.</p>

  <h2 id="google">Google user data</h2>
  <p>opendesk uses Google APIs in these ways, and only when you choose to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sign in with Google.</strong> We receive your name, email address, profile picture and Google account identifier, and use them only to create and sign in to your opendesk account.</li>
    <li><strong>Connect a Gmail or Google Workspace mailbox.</strong> An admin of a workspace can connect a mailbox so the replies agents write are sent from its address. With your permission, opendesk uses the Gmail API only to send those replies, and receives the account's email address to show which account is connected. With this send-only connection, opendesk does not read, search, change or delete the email in your mailbox through Google; the email that appears in opendesk is email you forward to it. We access only the account you connect, and only to provide this feature.</li>
    <li><strong>Read a support mailbox directly (Collab plan).</strong> An admin can instead connect a support mailbox so opendesk reads its inbox through the Gmail API (the <code>gmail.modify</code> permission). opendesk reads new email in the inbox to turn it into tickets, sends replies from the address, and, as the workspace's settings say, marks those emails read, adds an <code>opendesk</code> label and archives them. It never deletes email.</li>
    <li><strong>Connect a Collab mail account (Collab plan).</strong> A member can connect their own Gmail or Google Workspace account to use it in opendesk as a mail app. With the <code>gmail.modify</code> permission opendesk reads the account's email, labels and threads, keeps them in sync, and makes the changes the member makes in opendesk at Google: marking read or unread, starring, labelling, archiving, moving to trash or spam, saving drafts and sending. It never deletes email for good, except a draft written in opendesk that the member discards. With the <code>gmail.settings.basic</code> permission, if the member grants it, opendesk reads and changes the account's signature, sender name and vacation responder when the member edits them in opendesk. A private account is visible only to that member, never to workspace admins or to anyone at opendesk; a shared account only to the people and teams it is shared with. This email is never sent to AI providers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>How we store it.</strong> Replies sent through Gmail, and email read from a support mailbox, are stored as messages in the tickets of the workspace that connected the mailbox, along with the email forwarded to it. Email from a Collab mail account is stored in that account in opendesk, encrypted at rest by our hosting providers, and is removed when the account is disconnected. OAuth tokens are encrypted with AES-GCM before they are stored, and are used only by the opendesk service to talk to Google on behalf of that mailbox.</p>
  <p><strong>Who sees it.</strong> Email from a connected mailbox is visible only to members of the workspace that connected it, and email from a Collab mail account only to the member who connected it or the people it is shared with. We do not share Google user data with anyone except the service providers that host the Service, under contracts that limit them to providing it to us. We do not transfer it to advertising platforms, data brokers or information resellers.</p>
  <p><strong>People at opendesk.</strong> Nobody at opendesk reads your Google user data unless you ask us to for support and give us permission, it is needed for security purposes such as investigating abuse, it is required to comply with the law, or the data is aggregated and anonymized for internal operations.</p>
  <p><strong>Limited Use.</strong> opendesk's use and transfer to any other app of information received from Google APIs will adhere to the <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements. We use Google user data only to provide and improve the user-facing features described above, never to serve advertisements, and never to train generalized AI or machine learning models.</p>
  <p><strong>Revoking access.</strong> Disconnect the mailbox in opendesk under Settings, Mailboxes, or the Collab mail account under its settings, which deletes the stored tokens. You can also remove opendesk's access at any time from your Google account at <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>. Messages already in opendesk stay in the workspace until they are deleted as described under <a href="#deletion">Deleting your data</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="microsoft">Microsoft user data</h2>
  <p>The same rules apply when you sign in with Microsoft or connect a Microsoft 365 or Outlook mailbox: we receive your name, email address and account identifier to sign you in. For a send-only mailbox, we use only the permission to send email from the account you connect, and its email address, to send the replies your team writes; we do not read the mailbox's contents through Microsoft. On the Collab plan, a support mailbox connected to read its inbox directly uses <code>Mail.ReadWrite</code> and <code>Mail.Send</code> to read new inbox email into tickets, send replies, and mark, categorize and archive those emails as the workspace's settings say. A Collab mail account uses <code>Mail.ReadWrite</code>, <code>Mail.Send</code> and <code>MailboxSettings.ReadWrite</code> to read and sync the account's email and folders both ways, send and save drafts, and read and change its automatic replies and categories when the member edits them. A private Collab account is visible only to the member who connected it, and Collab email is never sent to AI providers. Email is never deleted for good, except a draft written in opendesk that the member discards. Tokens are encrypted, data is not used for advertising or to train AI models, and you can disconnect at any time in opendesk or from your Microsoft account.</p>

  <h2 id="legal-bases">Legal bases</h2>
  <p>Where the GDPR or UK GDPR applies, we rely on:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contract:</strong> to provide the Service you signed up for.</li>
    <li><strong>Legitimate interests:</strong> to secure the Service, prevent abuse, understand aggregate usage and improve it, and answer your messages. We balance these against your rights.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent:</strong> where we ask for it, for example to connect a mailbox through Google or Microsoft. You can withdraw it at any time.</li>
    <li><strong>Legal obligation:</strong> to keep billing records and respond to lawful requests.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="sharing">Sharing</h2>
  <p>We share personal data only:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>with service providers who host and run parts of the Service for us, under contracts that limit them to doing so;</li>
    <li>within your workspace, as you and your workspace's admins configure it, including with integrations and webhooks your workspace sets up;</li>
    <li>when the law requires it, after checking the request is valid, and telling the affected customer when we are allowed to;</li>
    <li>to protect the rights, safety and security of our users, the public or us;</li>
    <li>with a successor if opendesk is involved in a merger or acquisition, under this policy, with notice to you.</li>
  </ul>


  <h2 id="transfers">International transfers</h2>
  <p>Our providers may process data outside your country, including in the United States. Where data from the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom or Switzerland is transferred, we rely on the European Commission's Standard Contractual Clauses, the UK addendum, or another lawful transfer mechanism.</p>

  <h2 id="retention">Retention</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account data</strong> is kept while your account exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Workspace content</strong>, including imported email, is kept until it is deleted in the workspace or the workspace is deleted.</li>
    <li><strong>Mailbox credentials and OAuth tokens</strong> are deleted when the mailbox is disconnected or deleted.</li>
    <li><strong>Collab mail accounts</strong>: the synced email, folders and credentials are deleted from opendesk when the account is disconnected or the workspace is deleted, and a private account also when its member leaves the workspace. A shared account whose member left stays for the workspace's admins to manage or disconnect. Nothing is deleted at the provider.</li>
    <li><strong>The realtime event log</strong>, used to replay updates to clients that reconnect, keeps entries for 7 days.</li>
    <li><strong>Request logs</strong> are kept for up to 30 days. <strong>Audit logs</strong> are kept for the life of the workspace so admins can review them.</li>
    <li><strong>Backups</strong> are kept for up to 30 days, after which deleted data is gone from them too.</li>
    <li><strong>Billing records</strong> are kept as long as tax and accounting law requires.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deletion">Deleting your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your account:</strong> delete it in the app under your profile settings. If you are the only owner of a workspace with other members, transfer ownership or delete the workspace first.</li>
    <li><strong>A workspace:</strong> its owner can delete it under workspace settings. Everything in it is removed: tickets, messages, attachments, contacts, articles, mailbox connections and settings.</li>
    <li><strong>A mailbox connection:</strong> an admin can disconnect it at any time, which deletes its stored credentials.</li>
    <li><strong>Individual records:</strong> agents can delete tickets, contacts and articles in the app, subject to their role.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Deleted data is removed, not hidden. It disappears from backups when they expire. You can also ask us to delete your data by writing to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>. If your data is in someone else's workspace, we will pass your request to them.</p>

  <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
  <p>We encrypt data in transit with TLS, rely on our providers' encryption at rest, and additionally encrypt credentials and secrets with AES-GCM. Passwords are hashed with Argon2id. Access inside a workspace is controlled by roles, and every request is limited to the caller's workspace. Our controls are described on the <a href="/security/">security page</a>, and the code is public at <a href={LINKS.github}>github.com/useopendesk/opendesk</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access the personal data we hold about you, correct it, delete it, restrict or object to how we use it, receive a copy in a portable format, and withdraw consent. California residents have the right to know, delete and correct, and not to be discriminated against for using these rights; we do not sell or share personal information for cross-context behavioral advertising.</p>
  <p>To use any of these rights, write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>. We will answer within one month, and may need to confirm your identity first. You can also complain to your local data protection authority.</p>

  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
  <p>This website sets no cookies. The app sets one essential cookie when you sign in, an HttpOnly refresh token scoped to the sign-in path, which keeps you signed in and is removed when you sign out. The chat widget stores a visitor token in the visitor's browser so conversations continue between visits. Cloudflare Turnstile, which protects sign-in and sign-up, may process device signals to tell people from bots. We use no advertising cookies.</p>

  <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
  <p>The Service is for businesses and is not directed at children under 16. We do not knowingly collect their data. If you believe a child has given us personal data, contact us and we will delete it.</p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
  <p>We will post any change to this policy on this page and update the date at the top. If a change is significant, we will tell account owners by email before it takes effect.</p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>{COMPANY.legalName}<br />{COMPANY.address}<br />Company number {COMPANY.number}, registered in {COMPANY.registeredIn}<br />ICO registration reference {COMPANY.ico}<br />Email: <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a></p>
  <p>You also have the right to complain to the Information Commissioner's Office at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">ico.org.uk</a>.</p>
  <p>For security reports, write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>.</p>
</Legal>
